Networking
The DNS over HTTPS (DoH) protocol allows the DNS queries to be transmitted over an encrypted HTTPS connection to a DNS resolver. 
This is to ensure that queries and responses cannot be easily noticed by anyone who is watching the network. 
DoH does not, however, provide privacy of ALL internet activity nor do they obscure ALL connections.
The DNS resolver can still be seen and other data can provide information about the user's activity.
DoH is helpful, but not a full security solution.
Resource: RFC 8484 - DNS Queries over HTTPS (DoH) - https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8484

Cybersecurity
Phishing resistant authentication adds an added layer of security to prevent users from being lured onto fraudulent sites that request user credentials. 
With a correct implementation of passkeys and security keys with an appropriate FIDO2/WebAuthn mechanism, phishing resistance can be achieved. 
NIST guidance deems passwords, SMS codes, and manually-entered codes from an authenticator app as non-phishing-resistant. 
With strong authentication, the risk of attackers being able to gain access to citizens' accounts can be mitigated for a national login service.
Resource: NIST SP 800-63B - Authentication and Authenticator Management - https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html

Data
The authority is required to abide by the Personal Data Protection Law of Oman, Royal Decree 6/2022 and its Executive Regulation. 
It should convey the way the personal data is used by citizens, and it should secure the data from unauthorized access. 
The authority should also honour people's rights, adhere to consents as necessary and report on data breaches as per applicable requirements.
Before collecting or sharing personal information, it should refer to the law and regulations.
Resource: Personal Data Protection - MTCIT - https://mtcit.gov.om/sectors/governance/personal
